{
  "dom_id": "step-q346760",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/6f753b0f958204f705f0d59610210ae20e8f3096f0a9847bf72e2d3ab43a6305.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is half its width. Given that the box's volume is $243$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{2})+2x(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
